### Precision Positioning with the Arduino `<Servo.h>` Library

Generating 50Hz pulses with microsecond accuracy by hand using `delayMicroseconds()` would block your entire program. Fortunately, the official Arduino `<Servo.h>` library uses **Hardware Timer 1** to generate perfect background servo pulses automatically!

```cpp
#include <Servo.h>

// Create a Servo object instance
Servo steeringServo;

// Pin 10 connects to the Orange/Yellow signal wire of the SG90 servo
const int servoPin = 10;

void setup() {
  // Attach the servo object to pin 10 (Timer 1 takes over pulse generation)
  steeringServo.attach(servoPin);

  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println(F("--- SG90 Precision Servo Initialized ---"));
}

void sweepSmoothly() {
  Serial.println(F("Sweeping smoothly from 0 to 180 degrees..."));
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 2) {
    steeringServo.write(angle);
    delay(15); // Allow mechanical gears time to reach target angle
  }

  delay(500);

  Serial.println(F("Sweeping smoothly back from 180 to 0 degrees..."));
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 2) {
    steeringServo.write(angle);
    delay(15);
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Step 1: Discrete position targeting
  Serial.println(F("TARGET: 0 degrees (Full Left)"));
  steeringServo.write(0);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println(F("TARGET: 90 degrees (Neutral Center)"));
  steeringServo.write(90);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println(F("TARGET: 180 degrees (Full Right)"));
  steeringServo.write(180);
  delay(1000);

  // Step 2: Smooth mechanical sweep
  sweepSmoothly();
  delay(1000);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `#include <Servo.h>`: Imports the standard Arduino servo library, which controls servos via background hardware timer interrupts.
- `steeringServo.attach(servoPin)`: Binds Pin 10 to the servo object and starts outputting 50Hz pulse trains immediately.
- `steeringServo.write(angle)`: Accepts an angle between `0` and `180` degrees and converts it into the exact microsecond pulse duration ($1000\,\mu\text{s} - 2000\,\mu\text{s}$).
- `delay(15)` in the sweep loop: Essential mechanical pacing! Servos take a finite physical time to turn (SG90 speed is approximately $0.1\text{ s} / 60^\circ$). Stepping the angle gradually prevents mechanical gear backlash.
